+
+## Developer diagnostics
+
+The homepage has independent connection checks for a hardware device on the
+reader’s LAN and a companion CLI on their machine. The example endpoints are
+`http://192.168.1.100:8080/api/status` and `http://127.0.0.1:45678/health`;
+replace them in the widget with your services’ actual HTTP(S) status URLs.
+No bundled daemon or particular CLI command is required.
+
+Each Connect button sends a single browser-side CORS GET, with no cookies,
+authorization headers, redirects, or server proxy. The endpoint should return
+HTTP 2xx and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <the page's origin>` (or `*` for a
+noncredentialed public status endpoint). Any response body is displayed as text,
+limited to 4 KB. Requests time out after 15 seconds and can be cancelled.
+
+Deploy the page over HTTPS. The widget uses the separate `local-network` and
+`loopback-network` permissions and declares `targetAddressSpace` on each request.
+Permission queries are optional: browsers that do not recognize them can still
+try a connection on a user click. HTTP access from an HTTPS page depends on
+browser support for Local Network Access; use a trusted HTTPS endpoint or a
+supporting browser when mixed-content rules block it. The service still needs
+standard CORS headers. No deprecated Private Network Access headers are required.
+
+Run the network logic checks without starting any servers:
+
+```sh
+node --test src/utils/diagnostics.test.ts
+```
